          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The dietary guidance for managing high blood pressure emphasizes a pattern of eating that includes a variety of healthy foods. This dietary approach highlights the importance of:\n\n- Vegetables\n- Fruits\n- Whole grains\n- Low-fat dairy\n- Poultry\n- Fish\n- Beans\n- Nuts\n- Vegetable oils\n\nAt the same time, it advises limiting certain foods such as saturated fats, full-fat dairy, fatty meats, sweets, and sugar-sweetened drinks. This is not just about adding one \"blood pressure-friendly\" food; instead, it's about the overall pattern of food consumed, which should change comprehensively for the best results. Also, reducing sodium intake can enhance the effects of this dietary approach (source: General clinical reference and dietary pattern guidance).\n\nI did not cover specific dietary examples or meals, as that level of detail is not available in the guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
